Plan2001's changes to 9front's own files, as patches against the source
upstream/9front.lock locks (COMMIT), applied in patches/9front/series's
order by tools/upstream (tools/tree, tools/treebranch).  Each is made with
git diff --full-index and names the exact blob it applies to and makes:
it applies only to that file, with no fuzz or offset, or the assembly
stops.  The patch is the only copy of a changed upstream file; this tree
has new files only, and one at an upstream path stops the assembly.  To
change a patched file, edit it in an assembled tree and make the patch
anew against the locked file (tools/upstream assemble checks it).
series gives each patch's class (fix, backport, wasm32, uefi,
integration) and upstream status; fixes are candidates to offer 9front
with the owner's consent.  tools/upstream check shows what a newer 9front
changes; tools/upstream update rebases the stack onto it (three-way: old
upstream, Plan2001's, new upstream) and stops on any conflict.

hjfs-auth.diff	hjfs/auth.c: validuser measured a name as n - p, which is
		never USERLEN or more, so any name passed and the strcpy()s
		after it overran name, lead (the stack) and memb (the heap);
		lookupuid and ingroup could read one past the end of their
		arrays when the uid was above every one (the binary search ends
		with i == n).  2026-10-02.

libc-9syscall-wasm32.diff
		libc/9syscall/mkfile: wasm32 has no system call instruction;
		its stubs are C, each a call of the platform's _trap with the
		call's number and its arguments in memory (3c's convention),
		compiled by $CC.  The other machines' assembler as it was;
		the recipe ends with the common rm, so a native machine's
		build does not take the wasm32 test's status.  2026-10-04.

libthread-wasm32.diff
		libthread/mkfile: wasm32's machine part is its own -
		wasm32/wasm32.c, main.c, sched.c (contexts, not longjmp) - in
		place of $objtype.c, main.c and sched.c; the others as they
		were.  2026-10-04.

inst-bootsetup-9fat.diff
		rc/bin/inst/bootsetup: the machine's other boot files,
		/$cputype/9fat/*, onto the boot FAT with the kernel and
		plan9.ini (wasm32: the kernel's boot file system and the page
		that is its firmware; no other machine has the directory);
		and not into plan9.ini what the firmware says each boot
		(wasm32's *sdWn=, its disks' registers; init=, iso=).
		Format FAT without a legacy boot sector and copy EFI loaders
		from the current architecture directory.

The libc and libthread ones are for building wasm32 with 9front's mkfiles, with this
tree's libc/wasm32/mkfile and libmp's, libsec's empty wasm32/mkfiles.
wasm32 is not in 9front's CPUS: mk installall builds the CPUS, and wasm32
is built by objtype=wasm32 mk install where it is wanted.


pc-memory-bootinfo.diff
		pc/memory.c: optional architecture callback supplies the BootInfo
		memory map. The ordinary pc e820/ramscan path remains unchanged
		when no callback is installed.

pc-screen-bootinfo.diff
		pc/screen.c: accept the shared BootInfo WIDTHxHEIGHTxSTRIDExDEPTH
		configuration as well as the stock WIDTHxHEIGHTxDEPTH form.

riow-spawn.diff
		riow.c: name its private window launcher spawnwindow, avoiding a
		declaration collision with libc's process-spawn interface.

arp-first-request.diff
		arp.c: a fresh entry has not sent a request. Backdate its request
		time by RETRANS_TIMER so early boot does not suppress the first
		ARP request and discard the first queued packet on the next send.

spawn-wasm32.diff
		The rc spawn fast path is enabled only for objtype=wasm32. Native
		rc continues to fork/exec with the native syscall interface.

bcm64-uefi-bootinfo.diff
		9/bcm64: the Raspberry Pi 4 kernel the Plan2001 EFI loader
		enters under TF-A and EDK2 (CONF=pi4efi, -DUEFI; uefi.c is
		new): mem.h's low area and KTZERO moved up by PHYSBASE, past
		the firmware's 0 - 0x400000; X0 kept as BootInfo v1's address
		(l.s); memory from BootInfo's map, configuration from
		plan9.ini and the device tree it carries, secondary CPUs and
		reset by PSCI (main.c, mem.c); smccall and hvccall (l.s).
		The stock pi3 and pi4 kernels are as they were, but for two
		fixes: an explicit rule for bootargs.$O from ../bcm/bootargs.c
		(with ../port/bootargs.c, the shared BootInfo arguments, mk
		finds two metarules and stops), and mem.c no longer unmaps
		KZERO's window above the image, which ../arm64/mmu.c's
		kmapram() now leaves to the initial map.  2026-11-24.

native-syscall-contract.diff
		port/mksystab uses the same architecture selector as libc.
		Shared bootargs is opt-in; board-local sources keep ownership.

syscall-tab.diff
		cmd/syscall/mkfile: tab.h from the same architecture selector
		as libc and the kernel (libc/9syscall/syscalls), not from
		sys.h's raw #define lines: mktab.awk took sys.h's #ifdef and
		#endif around wasm32's SPAWN as calls, and syscall(1) did not
		compile.  spawn is in wasm32's table only.  2026-11-24.

sdvirtio-scsi-lun.diff
		pc/sdvirtio.c, port/sdvirtio10.c: virtio-scsi addressed unit
		n as target n, logical unit 0, and took max_target (the
		highest target id) as the number of targets, so a disk at
		another LUN (target 0 LUN 1 on Oracle Cloud) or at the highest
		target was not found, and a controller with max_target 0 not
		used.  On enable the driver asks each target for its logical
		units (REPORT LUNS to LUN 0); LUN 0 of target t stays unit t,
		the other LUNs take the free unit numbers in target and LUN
		order, and every request carries its unit's own target and
		LUN.  A LUN is a unit only when INQUIRY finds a device there:
		targets report LUN 0 without one (QEMU's 'QEMU TARGET',
		peripheral qualifier 1, type 1Fh).  Targets without REPORT LUNS get LUN 0 as before; virtio
		block devices are unchanged.  2026-11-24.

kbdfs-cooked-input.diff
		aux/kbdfs: ctlproc handed each cooked-mode rune to lineproc
		with nbsend on the unbuffered cook channel.  After a newline
		lineproc's nbsendp(conschan) yields (also after ^D and after
		the 256th rune of a long line), so when the next rune was
		already in runechan nobody was receiving and it was dropped:
		the first character of a line typed or pasted after another
		(serial console: r00001 arrived as 00001, ^D was lost) and the
		257th of a long one.  ctlproc now blocks in send; lineproc
		never waits for it.  2026-11-25.

kbdfs-serial-console.diff
		aux/kbdfs -c: the runes it reads from its console file go to
		cons even while kbd is open, so the serial line stays a console
		of its own when rio takes the keyboard, instead of becoming
		rio's keyboard (rio's per-window queue of 32 keys and kbdchan's
		nbsendp dropped a serial paste's characters).  kbdfs(8).
		In raw mode a console-file rune no longer goes to conschan
		with nbsendp (128 runes, then dropped): ctlproc holds it,
		reading no more from consfd, until a reader takes it, and
		goes on taking ctlchan's messages.  2026-11-25.

serial-console-shell.diff
		boot/bootrc starts kbdfs with -c.  rc/bin/termrc: with
		$console set, a shell of its own on the serial line, outside
		rio, started after termrc's mounts (dns), once
		(/env/serialshell) and again when it ends:
		a kbdfs of its own reads /srv/cons raw (holding rawon), edits
		lines and echoes; DEL abandons the shell's command line, and
		the restarting loop, an rc of its own (rc's forked subshells
		exit on any note they catch), ignores it.  rc/bin/console:
		in rio, with that shell running, the console window is an
		ordinary one, not a second shell teed to the line.
		2026-11-25.

libwebauthn-mkfile.diff
		sys/src/mkfile: libwebauthn among LIBS, the relying party's
		WebAuthn checks (webauthn.h) that aux/ethernetd and
		auth/accountd share.  2026-11-25.

rcpu-script-read.diff
		rc/bin/service/tcp17019, !il17019 and rconnect's aan server:
		the script was run while read -c was still reading it from
		the connection, and its first line, mount /fd/0, reads the
		same connection.  When the rest of the script arrived after
		the mount had started (the client's script in several TLS
		records, a slow link), the mount took script text as its
		Rversion and read -c took the client's Rversion: both waited
		for ever.  The whole script is now read before it runs.
		2026-11-26.

tcp-splice.diff
		ip/tcp.c: a local connection's two conversations are spliced
		(tcb->bypass) and tcpclose() hung up the other side's receive
		queue without checking that it still pointed back: when that
		conversation had been closed and reused for a new connection,
		the new one lost its data and read end of file mid-reply (an
		auth server's 113-byte ticket reply arriving as 57 bytes while
		other connections closed).  Writing to an already closed other
		side dropped the data silently; it is now Ehungup, as qbwrite()
		gives.  tcpacktimer() no longer takes the Conv lock while
		qsetbypass() flushes during tcpsplice() (a deadlock).  These are
		9front's 026e87832, 890656a50 and d683e5c0c.  2026-11-26.

touchscreen.diff
		port/devmouse.c keeps platform.js's touch bits (Touchbit,
		Menureq, Longreq, Touchstart) in /dev/mouse; rio/rio.c turns a
		tap on the desktop into its persistent button 3 menu and a long
		press into its button 2 menu, and strips the bits before a
		window sees the event.  WASM32's mobile Rio.

efi-loader.diff
		boot/efi: Plan2001's UEFI loader - BootInfo v1 for the kernel
		(x64.s, aa64.s, efi.c, sub.c), 9front a.out and restricted ELF64
		kernels, framebuffer stage marks, ARM64 platform selection
		(efiplatform=qemu-virt|rpi4, efibootmap, efiscratch), and an
		install target for /amd64 instead of /386.

pc64-uefi-bootinfo.diff
		9/pc64: the kernel entered by the EFI loader with BootInfo v1
		(l.s's _efi64 entry at KTZERO, main.c's BootInfo memory, screen
		and configuration, mem.h, trap.c, fns.h) and mkfile rules that
		make sd53c8xx.i and the etherm10g firmware tables here rather
		than in the pruned ../pc.
